  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated thunderstorms possible across \nnorthwest areas Thursday afternoon. Isolated/scattered showers \nand thunderstorms possible regionwide Thursday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThunderstorms will develop across Quebec province Thursday\nafternoon in advance of an approaching cold front. These\nthunderstorms could begin to reach northwest portions of the \nforecast area Thursday afternoon. The remnants of these showers\nand thunderstorms will then cross the region Thursday night. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be strong with gusty winds and small\nhail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front crosses the area Friday on the leading edge of a \nfull latitude trough pushing into the region, with the added \nsupport of a northern stream shortwave pushing through the \ntrough around that time. Current guidance shows that the cold\nfront is expected to be just off to our west on Friday morning and\nreach central Maine by early afternoon as convection develops \nalong and ahead of it. This should focus the area of \nthunderstorms towards central Maine, eastern Aroostook, and far \ninterior Downeast. Current model guidance shows the potential \nfor some strong to possibly severe storms with instability of up\nto 1500 J/kg and deep-layer shear around 30 kts driven by \nelongated, straight hodographs. Mid-level lapse rates are also \nrelatively favorable which is typically a limiting factor for \nour severe threat. While severe potential exists there are still\nsome outstanding questions that could modify the threat. First,\nhow much cloud cover will be left over from showers and storms \nthe night before? This could change our low-level thermodynamics\nand instability profile. Second, where exactly will the cold \nfront be during the afternoon? This will determine where storms \nset up. Third, how far inland will the marine layer penetrate? \nThe further north the marine layer penetrates, the lower the \nstorm risk along the coast.",
